<section>
  <p class="eyebrow">Speaker A · exact canon excerpt</p>
  <h2>Article Eight</h2>
  <blockquote class="reading"><p>The whole of the law is that what action harms¹ none is a right; that what is done to preserve life according to and in balance with nature² is a right; that what is done to resist coercion³, or aid another to do the same, is a right; that what is done in the presence of informed⁴ consent⁵ is a right.</p></blockquote>
  <div class="grid">
    <article class="panel"><h3>Plain orientation</h3><p>Article Eight defines rights by relation to harm, life, nature, coercion, consent, and tools of survival. It is less a list of permissions than a test for whether action preserves agency and life.</p></article>
    <article class="panel"><h3>Central tension</h3><p>The article protects liberty, but every term is difficult: harm, nature, coercion, informed, consent. Article Seven’s definition work is required before Article Eight can be applied safely.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>
<section>
  <p class="eyebrow">Speaker B · footnote conversation</p>
  <h2>What the notes keep from collapsing</h2>
  <article class="panel turn note"><span class="status status-interpretation">note ¹ · harm</span><p><strong>Canon note:</strong> Without debate, freedom is dead.</p><p><strong>Function:</strong> prevents harm from being declared by authority alone. The right to debate keeps freedom from being replaced by accusation.</p></article>
  <article class="panel turn note"><span class="status status-interpretation">note ² · nature</span><p><strong>Canon note:</strong> a complex system requiring consumption of the dead for continued participation.</p><p><strong>Function:</strong> rejects sanitized nature. Life persists through cycles, dependency, death, and transformation.</p></article>
  <article class="panel turn note"><span class="status status-interpretation">note ³ · coercion</span><p><strong>Canon note:</strong> save for parents or appointed guardians of children or invalids.</p><p><strong>Function:</strong> names a hard exception: care for those who cannot safely exercise full independent agency may include constraint, but that exception is dangerous and must remain bounded.</p></article>
  <article class="panel turn note"><span class="status status-interpretation">note ⁴ · informed</span><p><strong>Canon note:</strong> a Myth as reality defies certainty.</p><p><strong>Function:</strong> keeps informed consent humble. No one knows everything; the duty is meaningful disclosure, not impossible omniscience.</p></article>
  <article class="panel turn note"><span class="status status-interpretation">note ⁵ · consent</span><p><strong>Canon note:</strong> sexuality, confined to Adults, respects no age limitations.</p><p><strong>Function:</strong> makes consent the governing boundary for adult intimacy while preserving adulthood as the threshold named by the canon.</p></article>
</section>
<section>
  <p class="eyebrow">Application</p>
  <h2>How this becomes public practice</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    <article class="card"><span class="status status-implemented">small group</span><h3>Rights test</h3><p>Ask whether an action harms, preserves life, resists coercion, or rests on meaningful consent. If the answer is unclear, return to definitions.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><span class="status status-implemented">institution</span><h3>Consent boundary</h3><p>Require meaningful disclosure, visible capacity, and a coercion check before treating permission as consent.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><span class="status status-implemented">self-governance</span><h3>Consent check</h3><p>Do not rely on technical permission where disclosure, capacity, dependency, or coercion makes consent false.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="panel">
  <p class="eyebrow">Handbook seed</p>
  <h2>Chapter section: Rights with living limits</h2>
  <p>Article Eight gives a rights test. A right is not merely what someone wants. A right is action that does not harm, preserves life in balance with nature, resists coercion, aids resistance to coercion, or occurs with informed consent.</p>
  <p>Applying this article requires definitions. Harm must be debated. Nature must be understood as a living system. Coercion must be identified without making care impossible. Consent must be meaningful despite incomplete knowledge.</p>
  <p>The work is practical: return unclear cases to Article Seven's definition discipline before declaring an action protected.</p>
</section>
<section class="panel">
  <p class="eyebrow">60–90 second script</p>
  <h2>Short video draft</h2>
  <p><strong>Title:</strong> What makes a right?</p>
  <p>Article Eight asks a hard question: what makes an action a right?</p>
  <p>The answer is not simply desire. An action is a right when it harms none, preserves life in balance with nature, resists coercion, aids another resisting coercion, or happens in the presence of informed consent.</p>
  <p>Every one of those words needs care. Harm must be debated. Nature is not clean and sentimental; it is a complex system. Coercion has exceptions for guardianship, but exceptions must be watched. Informed consent is never perfect, because reality defeats certainty.</p>
  <p>The article does not make those words easy. It makes them unavoidable: rights need shared definitions before they can guide public action.</p>
</section>
